    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed"><strong>An undersized actuator on a ball valve is not a slow failure — it is a valve that stops in mid-travel, a plant that trips on a stuck shutoff, and a site visit to swap hardware that was chosen from the wrong torque column.</strong> Ball valve torque is not a single catalog number: it climbs with valve size, pressure class and seat material, and the breakaway torque you need at the start of the stroke is almost always higher than the torque needed to keep the ball moving. Get the sizing method right and a rack and pinion actuator rated for the job opens and closes a 6-inch ball valve in under a second for years; get it wrong and the same actuator stalls at the worst possible moment.</p>
